 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T12:45+08:00",
 "answer": "**No, it is not safe to exercise outdoors in Jurong.** You should move your workout indoors due to **hazardous haze levels** and incoming afternoon weather conditions.\n\n### 1. Severe Haze & Unhealthy Air Quality (Primary Hazard)\nSingapore is experiencing significant transboundary smoke haze, with the Western region (including Jurong) being hit the hardest:\n* **1-Hour PM2.5 (Immediate Exposure):** Readings in Western Singapore have spiked to **158 µg/m³**, placing Jurong in the **High (Band 3)** category.\n* **24-Hour PSI:** Island-wide readings are in the **Unhealthy** range (139–184), and the National Environment Agency (NEA) forecasts the PSI to stay between **150 and 230 (Unhealthy to Very Unhealthy)** throughout the day.\n* **Official NEA / MOH Advisory:** Under High 1-hour PM2.5 readings, **even healthy adults are advised to avoid strenuous outdoor physical exertion**. Vulnerable groups (the elderly, pregnant women, children, and those with chronic heart or lung conditions) should avoid all outdoor activities entirely.\n\n### 2. Midday Heat & Afternoon Thunderstorms\n* **Heat Stress:** Midday temperatures in the Jurong area are sitting around **30°C–32°C (86°F–90°F)**, with high humidity pushing the \"feels like\" temperature to **36°C–39°C (97°F–102°F)**. \n* **Lightning Risk:** The Meteorological Service Singapore (MSS) has forecast **thundery showers over western Singapore** for the afternoon, which carries a high risk of cloud-to-ground lightning.\n\n### Recommendation\nOpt for an **indoor, air-conditioned workout** (with windows closed and an air purifier running if exercising at home). If you plan to go out later, check the live 1-hour PM2.5 readings for the West region and lightning alerts on the **myENV app** or [haze.gov.sg](https://www.haze.gov.sg) before heading out.",
